---
import KickoffSession from "../../components/KickoffSession.astro";
---

<KickoffSession
    documentTitle="The record travels with the patient. | Canada2080"
    description="Pyn Lim on patient-controlled health records at the Canada2080 kickoff."
    eyebrow="Sovereign AI · 1:20 p.m."
    headline="The record travels with the patient."
    name="Pyn Lim"
    bio="Pyn Lim is a medical-aesthetics trainer and an AI-education collaborator. Her work treats patient data sovereignty and fragmented health records as a continuity-of-care problem."
    profileUrl="https://www.linkedin.com/in/pynlim/"
    podiumSrc="/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5392.jpg"
    podiumAlt="Pyn Lim on the screen beside her slide, Patient data sovereignty."
    photos={[
        {
            src: "/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5392.jpg",
            alt: "Pyn Lim on the screen beside her slide, Patient data sovereignty.",
            caption: "On the screen. Patient data sovereignty.",
        },
    ]}
>
    <p class="lede">
        Patients leave a clinic without a portable record of what was done to
        the body. The clinic keeps a file. The next clinician may not.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            She has worked for nearly two decades across aesthetic medicine,
            aesthetic surgery, and postgraduate medical education.
        </li>
        <li>
            She cited a figure that post-care capture is about 60 percent. That
            figure is hers. The next clinician, especially after travel, may
            not know the product, the implant, or the procedure.
        </li>
        <li>
            Cases she named: fragmented vaccination booklets, aesthetic
            procedures, implants, and medical tourism, where the clinic abroad
            is hard to reach.
        </li>
        <li>
            The record she proposed is controlled by the patient and
            authenticated by the clinician. Patients do not edit clinical
            entries. Corrections are signed, dated additions that keep the
            original.
        </li>
        <li>
            A private assistant may organize the timeline, translate jargon,
            prepare a summary for a new clinician, and flag a drug or
            supplement interaction. Every output has to point at a verified
            source. It does not diagnose or prescribe.
        </li>
        <li>
            A related project is starting in Ireland. She invited a
            Canada-Europe link, and she asked for demonstrations that produce
            evidence rather than promises.
        </li>
    </ul>
</KickoffSession>
